  notification it produced, you now have two copies of the same truth that can disagree — change the due date and the
  stored notification is a lie.

  [1mA — Pure derived panel.[0m No notification entities at all. On load, one function maps tasks → attention items. Nothing
  to persist, nothing to migrate, cannot ever be stale. [3mCost:[0m no dismiss, no read/unread — the panel nags about the same
  overdue task forever, which is how people learn to ignore a panel. Trivial.

  [1mB — Derived + a dismissal ledger.[0m Same pure function, plus a small persisted set of keys you've acknowledged
  ([38;5;153mtaskId:rule:dueDate[39m). Dismissal survives reloads; changing the due date naturally produces a new key, so it re-fires
  — correct behavior for free. Also gives you "new since you last looked" by storing one timestamp. [3mCost:[0m one extra
  stored structure and a key-design decision. Moderate.

  [1mC — Stored notification log.[0m Each firing writes a record; full history, read/unread flags. [3mCost:[0m duplicates derivable
  state, needs reconciliation when tasks change, and needs pruning. Real history is the payoff, which a solo task app
  doesn't obviously need. High.

  [1mRecommendation: B.[0m A is too thin to stay useful past week one — an un-dismissable panel becomes wallpaper. C pays a
  permanent correctness tax for history you probably won't read. B keeps the single source of truth in the task data and
  persists only the one thing that genuinely can't be derived: what you've already seen.

  Sequencing is still as I proposed — tasks and storage first with a single mutation path, notifications second on top.
  I'll fold that into the design.
